  "level": 2,
  "question": {
    "text": "A microscope was initially placed in air (refractive index <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn></math>). It is then immersed in oil (refractive index <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math>). For a light whose wavelength in air is $\\lambda$, calculate the change of microscope&#39;s resolving power due to oil and choose the correct option",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "uTqT9",
      "text": "Resolving power will be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></math> in the oil than it was in the air",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "K5gtR",
      "text": "Resolving power will be twice in the oil than it was in the air.",
      "isCorrect": true,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "wgFAQ",
      "text": "Resolving power will be four times in the oil than it was in the air.",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "O4PgD",
      "text": "Resolving power will be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math> in the oil than it was in the air.",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>Resolving power of microscope in air is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mo>.</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mrow><mi>air</mi><mo>&#160;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>&#956;</mi><mi>air</mi></msub><mi>sin</mi><mi>&#952;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>22</mn><mi>&#955;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>sin</mi><mi>&#952;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>22</mn><mi>&#955;</mi></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mo>.</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mrow><mi>oil</mi><mo>&#160;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>&#956;</mi><mi>oil</mi></msub><mi>sin</mi><mi>&#952;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>22</mn><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mrow><mi>oil</mi><mo>&#160;</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>sin</mi><mi>&#952;</mi><mfenced><mn>2</mn></mfenced></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>22</mn><mi>&#955;</mi></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mfenced><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mo>.</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow></mfenced><mi>oil</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mfenced><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mo>.</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow></mfenced><mi>air</mi></msub><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>2</mn></math></p><p>Thus, resolving power in oil is twice the resolving power in air.</p></blockquote>",
